1
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,525
This is Priya.

2
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:12,233
She has just paid two hundred rupees for a train ticket, from her banking app.

3
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:14,052
Payment successful.

4
00:00:14,052 --> 00:00:19,035
Behind that screen, the bank's database sent back two words: UPDATE 1.

5
00:00:19,035 --> 00:00:22,167
Meaning: done. Saved. It will stay that way.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,700
One millisecond later, the power went out.

7
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:31,233
So, is Priya's money safe?

8
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,213
It is. But here's the twist.

9
00:00:35,213 --> 00:00:40,892
When the bank said done, her new balance had not been saved anywhere permanent.

10
00:00:40,892 --> 00:00:41,467
Not yet.

11
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,386
So how can a bank promise something it hasn't saved?

12
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:51,994
This happens every time you tap Pay. It takes a few milliseconds.

13
00:00:51,994 --> 00:00:53,767
Let's slow it right down.

14
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,496
Every computer works a lot like a restaurant kitchen.

15
00:01:11,496 --> 00:01:16,367
This one belongs to Priya's bank, and it's busy, even late at night.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,063
It has only four work lamps.

17
00:01:22,063 --> 00:01:25,600
A chef can only work while a lamp shines on her.

18
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:31,205
There are more chefs than lamps, so the lamps keep hopping from chef to chef.

19
00:01:31,205 --> 00:01:32,733
Everyone gets a turn.

20
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,633
No lamp, no work. Remember that.

21
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,711
This is the fridge. Everything in here is quick to grab.

22
00:01:44,711 --> 00:01:49,367
But the moment the power goes out, the fridge forgets everything.

23
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,935
And this is the storeroom. Anything in here survives a power cut.

24
00:01:56,935 --> 00:01:59,267
But it's a long, slow walk away.

25
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:11,700
“Ask me first. Always.”

26
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:15,011
And this is the Manager. He runs the building.

27
00:02:15,011 --> 00:02:19,833
Nobody touches a lamp, a shelf or the storeroom without asking him.

28
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,290
Keep your eye on two places: the fridge and the storeroom.

29
00:02:27,290 --> 00:02:31,238
This whole story is about which one Priya's money is in.

30
00:02:31,238 --> 00:02:34,200
We'll keep score at the top of the screen.

31
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,955
Priya's payment arrives at the door of the bank's database.

32
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:50,574
It's called PostgreSQL.

33
00:02:50,574 --> 00:02:54,588
Picture it as a catering company that rents this kitchen.

34
00:02:54,588 --> 00:02:56,067
Its staff wear coral.

35
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:05,259
The Host greets every new customer, but he never cooks.

36
00:03:05,259 --> 00:03:09,433
Instead, he asks the Manager for an exact copy of himself.

37
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:13,700
“Manager: copy me, please.”

38
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:21,800
“Hi Priya! I'm all yours.”

39
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,871
That copy becomes Mira: a brand new chef, just for Priya.

40
00:03:25,871 --> 00:03:30,800
Computer people call her a process. Making the copy is called a fork.

41
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,270
One chef per customer has a big upside: if another chef burns something,

42
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:37,733
Mira's work is safe.

43
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:47,702
No order yet, so Mira dozes off, and her lamp goes to someone busier.

44
00:03:47,702 --> 00:03:50,167
Computer people call this waiting.

45
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:54,583
Then the order arrives.

46
00:03:54,583 --> 00:03:59,633
It looks scary, but it only says: take two hundred rupees from Priya.

47
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:04,000
“Mira! Wake up!”

48
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,200
The Manager wakes her. She's ready to go, but she still needs a lamp.

49
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:13,300
There. Lamp on. Now she can work.

50
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:18,900
“Where does Priya's account live?”

51
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:21,743
First, Mira has to find Priya's account.

52
00:04:21,743 --> 00:04:26,505
The bank keeps an index, just like the index at the back of a book.

53
00:04:26,505 --> 00:04:28,567
It says: Priya is on page 42.

54
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:30,800
“Page 42!”

55
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,539
Page 42. But where is it right now? In the fridge?

56
00:04:34,539 --> 00:04:36,333
Or out in the storeroom?

57
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:45,300
She checks the company's own shelf in the fridge first.

58
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:46,567
Not there.

59
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
“Manager, can you fetch page 42?”

60
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,194
The Manager keeps a shelf of his own in the fridge, too.

61
00:04:56,194 --> 00:04:57,300
Not there either.

62
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:59,900
“The storeroom? Ugh. Long walk.”

63
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,713
So the Manager has to walk all the way to the storeroom.

64
00:05:03,713 --> 00:05:07,185
Mira can't do a thing until he's back, so she naps,

65
00:05:07,185 --> 00:05:09,500
and her lamp goes to another chef.

66
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,104
How long is this walk?

67
00:05:11,104 --> 00:05:16,500
If every move under a lamp took one second, this one trip would take days.

68
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:27,674
He's back with page 42, and puts it on the company shelf.

69
00:05:27,674 --> 00:05:32,533
Look at the score: Priya's account is in the fridge now, saying 500.

70
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,500
“Hands off, everyone. Writing!”

71
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:50,600
Now Mira makes the change, very carefully.

72
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:55,402
First she stamps Priya's card: mine, until order 812 is finished.

73
00:05:55,402 --> 00:06:00,075
That stamp is a lock. Anyone else who wants this card has to wait.

74
00:06:00,075 --> 00:06:01,067
Remember that.

75
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:07,077
Then, instead of rubbing out 500, she writes a brand new card: 300.

76
00:06:07,077 --> 00:06:09,333
The old card stays where it is.

77
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,400
“Still counting… Priya: 500”

78
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:24,191
Why keep the old card? Because of Leela.

79
00:06:24,191 --> 00:06:28,866
She's halfway through adding up every account for a monthly report.

80
00:06:28,866 --> 00:06:32,913
If Priya's 500 turned into 300 in the middle of her count,

81
00:06:32,913 --> 00:06:34,867
her totals would not add up.

82
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:40,273
So Leela keeps reading the old card, Mira writes the new one,

83
00:06:40,273 --> 00:06:42,400
and nobody waits for anybody.

84
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:46,233
The old card will need cleaning up later.

85
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:57,707
Now, the most important habit in this kitchen.

86
00:06:57,707 --> 00:07:00,300
It's the secret to the whole story.

87
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:05,831
Before any change counts, it must be written in the company notebook.

88
00:07:05,831 --> 00:07:09,833
Not the page itself, just a short note about the change.

89
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:16,622
This is the first change to page 42 since the last checkpoint,

90
00:07:16,622 --> 00:07:19,480
the last time everything was fully saved.

91
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:23,245
So Mira also copies the entire page into the notebook.

92
00:07:23,245 --> 00:07:26,033
Seems like overkill. Remember it anyway.

93
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,000
Then the note itself: page 42, Priya, now 300, order 812.

94
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:38,198
But look at the score: the notebook is still in the fridge.

95
00:07:38,198 --> 00:07:40,967
Nothing has reached the storeroom yet.

96
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:48,200
She sticks a reminder on page 42: changed, but not saved.

97
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:58,700
“Cashback time!”

98
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:02,271
Meanwhile, Arjun, one of the chefs at the counter,

99
00:08:02,271 --> 00:08:07,700
has an order for the very same account: a hundred rupees cashback for Priya.

100
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:14,833
He finds Mira's stamp: order 812, not finished.

101
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:18,800
“Seriously? Fine. I'll wait.”

102
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,300
“I read 500”

103
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:26,233
Picture it without the stamp. Both chefs read 500.

104
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:37,100
Mira writes 300. Then Arjun writes 600. Mira's payment just vanished.

105
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:42,000
The train company never gets paid. Engineers call this a lost update.

106
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,533
That's why Arjun waits.

107
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:57,810
Quick recap. Priya's new balance, 300, exists only in the fridge.

108
00:08:57,810 --> 00:08:59,867
One power cut, and it's gone.

109
00:08:59,867 --> 00:09:03,200
So how can the bank tell Priya done, right now?

110
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:08,500
Mira writes one final note: order 812, finished.

111
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:15,957
Here's the trick. She doesn't carry page 42 to the storeroom.

112
00:09:15,957 --> 00:09:21,400
A payment can touch pages all over the place, and there are thousands of them.

113
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:25,231
But the notes all sit together, on one notebook page.

114
00:09:25,231 --> 00:09:29,133
So she only sends the notebook. One small, quick trip.

115
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:32,600
“Manager! Notebook to the storeroom. Now, please.”

116
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:36,300
She asks the Manager to save the notebook to disk.

117
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:37,707
And then she waits.

118
00:09:37,707 --> 00:09:42,300
Every payment you have ever made has waited for this one trip.

119
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,700
“Done with #814!”

120
00:09:47,700 --> 00:09:50,710
Two other chefs finish at the same moment.

121
00:09:50,710 --> 00:09:54,365
The Manager takes all their notes in the same trip.

122
00:09:54,365 --> 00:09:56,300
That's called group commit.

123
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:02,446
The notes are in the storeroom.

124
00:10:02,446 --> 00:10:07,733
Now, even if the lights go out, Priya's payment can be rebuilt from them.

125
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,600
“Done. Safe on disk.”

126
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:27,133
Only now does Mira mark order 812 as done, in the company ledger.

127
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:35,031
“Finally! 300 plus 100 is 400.”

128
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:38,229
Order 812 is finished, so Arjun wakes up.

129
00:10:38,229 --> 00:10:42,157
He sees the new 300, and adds his cashback on top: 400.

130
00:10:42,157 --> 00:10:44,300
His order is not finished yet.

131
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:53,367
And Mira tells Priya: done. UPDATE 1.

132
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:58,600
Which is exactly where our story began.

133
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:02,767
And then, the lights go out.

134
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,616
Look inside the storeroom. Mira's notes are here. And page 42?

135
00:11:10,616 --> 00:11:11,733
Still says 500.

136
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:20,708
The 300 only ever lived in the fridge.

137
00:11:20,708 --> 00:11:24,033
And the fridge has just forgotten everything.

138
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:28,733
So, did the bank just lie to Priya?

139
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:47,871
When the power comes back, a recovery chef opens the notebook and replays every

140
00:11:47,871 --> 00:11:48,967
note, in order.

141
00:11:56,100 --> 00:12:00,567
First, it fetches page 42 from the storeroom. It says 500.

142
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:07,160
Then it finds the full-page copy Mira made. Remember it?

143
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:10,567
Page 42 comes back exactly as she left it: 300.

144
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:17,667
Order 812: done. Priya's money is safe. The notebook kept the promise.

145
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:20,894
And Arjun's cashback?

146
00:12:20,894 --> 00:12:25,447
It was never finished, and its note never reached the storeroom.

147
00:12:25,447 --> 00:12:28,933
So it simply never happened. His app tries again.

148
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:34,652
Now imagine the lights went out a moment earlier,

149
00:12:34,652 --> 00:12:38,567
while the Manager was still walking with the notebook.

150
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:47,600
“Payment failed. Try again?”

151
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:49,622
Then Priya never hears done.

152
00:12:49,622 --> 00:12:55,400
Her app shows an error, her balance is still 500, and she simply taps Pay again.

153
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:59,867
No promise was made, so no promise was broken.

154
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,738
That's the whole secret.

155
00:13:03,738 --> 00:13:09,533
The bank only says done once the note about the change is safe in the storeroom.

156
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:26,717
Later that night, the clean-up crew arrives.

157
00:13:26,717 --> 00:13:32,029
Every few minutes, the Supervisor makes sure every changed page really gets

158
00:13:32,029 --> 00:13:33,800
carried to the storeroom.

159
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:37,800
“Manager: save page 42 to disk.”

160
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:51,696
What if the lights go out halfway through this delivery?

161
00:13:51,696 --> 00:13:54,835
Page 42 could land half old, half new. Torn.

162
00:13:54,835 --> 00:13:56,833
A short note can't fix that.

163
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:03,900
But the full-page copy can. That's exactly why Mira made it.

164
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:09,058
This time all goes well.

165
00:14:09,058 --> 00:14:13,967
Look at the score: page 42 is finally in the storeroom, saying 300.

166
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:21,700
And old notebook pages, no longer needed, can finally be thrown away.

167
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,700
“Nobody needs this old one now.”

168
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:39,231
Last, the Cleaner sweeps away the old 500 card.

169
00:14:39,231 --> 00:14:41,033
Nobody needs it anymore.

170
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:49,469
This whole story rests on trust.

171
00:14:49,469 --> 00:14:53,935
When the Manager says it's in the storeroom, it has to be true.

172
00:14:53,935 --> 00:14:57,267
In 2018, engineers found that it wasn't always.

173
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:01,900
“Manager: save page 88, please.”

174
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:05,100
“Disk error!”

175
00:15:05,100 --> 00:15:07,725
A delivery to the storeroom failed.

176
00:15:07,725 --> 00:15:10,500
The Manager reported the error, once.

177
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:18,542
Then, on Linux, he could quietly mark the page as fine,

178
00:15:18,542 --> 00:15:20,967
and later drop it from his shelf.

179
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:23,900
“Try again?”

180
00:15:23,900 --> 00:15:24,800
“Try again?”

181
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:25,200
“All good!”

182
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:29,571
PostgreSQL asked again. This time the Manager said all good.

183
00:15:29,571 --> 00:15:32,267
The page never reached the storeroom.

184
00:15:34,700 --> 00:15:39,535
Today, if this ever happens, PostgreSQL stops on purpose, restarts,

185
00:15:39,535 --> 00:15:42,567
and rebuilds everything from the notebook.

186
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:51,867
A promise is only as strong as the promises underneath it.

187
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:58,467
Every character tonight was a real part of your computer, and of PostgreSQL.

188
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:23,903
One more thing.

189
00:16:23,903 --> 00:16:29,200
Tonight, every time Mira needed the storeroom, she had to stop and wait.

190
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:33,100
“Pages 43 to 50, please. I’ll keep working.”

191
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:37,419
Since PostgreSQL 18, for big jobs like reading a whole table,

192
00:16:37,419 --> 00:16:43,367
PostgreSQL can ask for many pages ahead of time, and keep working while they arrive.
